We can want the machines to help and still report a machineʼs failure
plainly. An ambition that can survive correction is more useful than
an image that must never crack.

This weekʼs handoff

1. Clean your output. Install a metadata stripper and make cleaning
   photos a habit before anything leaves your devices.
2. Start the archive. Download one Kiwix collection and the manuals
   your household or co-op actually needs. Put a copy on a second
   drive, then open it on the device youʼd use during an outage.
3. Make one useful thing for someone else. Offer a food group a
   simple choice: a corrected public schedule, a checked translation,
   a clear page on how to ask for help, or a short account of one
   repair. Let them choose, including “none of those.” Finish it, and
   confirm whoʼll keep it current.



Precedent P-20: An Argument in Circulation
(Philadelphia and London, 1776)
Thomas Paine had been in America barely a year. He was a stay-
makerʼs son and a twice-dismissed excise officer: no office, no for-
tune, no name worth borrowing. What he had was a printer and a
way of writing that ordinary people could read aloud in a tavern.






